  "name": "Take While the Numbers Are Small",
  "tool_name": null,
  "description": "TakeWhile stops at the first item that fails the predicate, unlike a filter, which would keep testing the rest. That difference is the reason to reach for it: it caps work rather than selecting from it. The expression binds each item as `item`.",
  "tags": [
    "example"
  ],
  "thumbnail": null,
  "thumbnail_url": null,
  "graph": {
    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": "intro-comment",
        "type": "nodetool.workflows.base_node.Comment",
        "data": {
          "comment": "# Take While the Numbers Are Small\n\nKeeps items from the start of a list until the first one fails a test, then stops. A filter would keep checking the rest, so TakeWhile caps work instead of selecting from it.\n\n**How it works:** \"Numbers\" holds 1, 2, 3, 40, 5. \"Fan out\" sends the items one at a time to \"While < 10\", which checks the expression item < 10. It stops at 40, so \"Prefix\" receives 1, 2, 3 and never sees the 5.\n\n**Try this:** Change the predicate on \"While < 10\" to item < 50 and check that the 5 now gets through."
        },
